+### TimescaleDB
+
+The same schema runs on PostgreSQL and TimescaleDB (that is what lets
+[switching](Installation#switching-between-postgresql-and-timescaledb)
+copy the data table by table). When the database has the `timescaledb`
+extension,
+[`app/db/timescale.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/db/timescale.py)
+adds, idempotently, as the last step of the `migrate` service
+(`python -m app.db.timescale` after `alembic upgrade head`; nothing on
+PostgreSQL):
+
+- **Hypertables** with one-day chunks for `machine_monitoring_samples`,
+  `machine_reachability_samples` and `endpoint_check_results` (existing
+  rows are moved into chunks the first time). Their primary keys are
+  `(id, <time column>)` on both databases, since a hypertable needs the
+  time column in every unique index.
+- **Compression** (TimescaleDB's columnstore, segmented by machine or
+  check, newest first) of chunks older than 7 days. The monitoring
+  samples are [downsampled](Host-Requirements#-disk-growth) first: with downsampling at N ≥ 7
+  days they compress at N + 1 days, so the nightly downsampling has gone
+  over a chunk before it is compressed, and on TimescaleDB downsampling
+  only looks at the days since. Changing the downsampling age (Settings
+  or the API) moves the compression age with it.
+- **Daily continuous aggregates** `reachability_daily` and
+  `endpoint_check_daily` (up and total probes per machine or check per
+  UTC day), refreshed hourly for the last three days and computed live
+  for anything newer. The [status page](Checks-and-Status-Page)'s uptime and day bars
+  read them instead of counting every sample.
+- **Retention stays the app's own** (Settings, per machine, audited): the
+  nightly purge first drops whole chunks older than the longest
+  retention any machine has (skipped while some machine keeps its
+  history forever), the same for the daily aggregates, then deletes
+  what a shorter per-machine retention still covers row by row, as on
+  PostgreSQL. No TimescaleDB retention job is used.
+
+A whole-application restore refreshes the aggregates afterwards;
+`scripts/restore.sh` uses `timescaledb_pre_restore()` /
+`timescaledb_post_restore()`; the move from PostgreSQL refreshes the
+aggregates once the data is in. Settings → Checks & retention and `GET
+/api/v1/settings` (`timescaledb`) show the version and compression ages.
+
